ior search step.\n\n---\n\n## 1) Total funding, key investors, latest valuation\n\n**Total funding raised**\n\n- Crunchbase News (Dec 2020) reports Calm raised **$75M Series C** at a **$2B valuation**, bringing **total funding to $217M** at that time.[1]  \n  URL: `https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/briefing-12-8-20/`\n- A series of Crunchbase articles shows the funding trajectory:\n  - **Series A**: $27M at $250M valuation (June 2018).[4]  \n    URL: `https://news.crunchbase.com/business/calm-raises-27m-series-a-amidst-growing-revenues-and-profitability/`\n  - **Series B**: $88M (Feb 2019) at **$1B valuation**, led by TPG Growth; existing investors include Insight Venture Partners and Sound Ventures.[2]  \n    URL: `https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/calm-raises-88m-series-b-to-become-mental-healths-first-unicorn/`\n  - **Series B extension**: additional $27M; overall funding at that point **$143M**.[6]  \n    URL: `https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/meditation-app-calm-adds-27m-extension-to-series-b/`\n- PitchBook lists Calm as having raised **$191M** (likely excluding some extension/secondary and/or later debt).[8]  \n  URL: `https://pitchbook.com/profiles/company/56204-65`\n- Sacra, an investor‑oriented research site, states Calm has raised a total of **$218M** across multiple funding rounds.[14]  \n  URL: `https://sacra.com/c/calm/`\n- GetLatka’s company profile similarly summarizes **$217M** in total funding across 4 rounds.[5]  \n  URL: `https://getlatka.com/companies/calm`\n\nGiven these, the best-supported range for **total equity/debt funding** is **~$217–218M**, with Crunchbase, Sacra and GetLatka agreeing around **$217M**.[1][5][14]\n\n**Key investors**\n\nFrom Crunchbase coverage of the Series A/B/C rounds:\n\n- **TPG Growth** (Series B lead).[2]  \n- **Insight Venture Partners / Insight Partners** (early and follow‑on investor).[2][3]  \n- **Lightspeed Venture Partners** (Series C lead, per coverage referenced in Crunchbase and GetLatka).[3][5]  \n- **Creative Artists Agency (CAA)** participating in the Series B round.[2]  \n- **Sound Ventures** (Ashton Kutcher’s fund).[2]  \n\nRelevant URLs:  \n- `https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/calm-raises-88m-series-b-to-become-mental-healths-first-unicorn/`[2]  \n- `https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/briefing-10-14-20/`[3]  \n- `https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/briefing-12-8-20/`[1]  \n- `https://getlatka.com/companies/calm`[5]\n\n**Latest valuation**\n\n- Crunchbase (Dec 2020) reports a **$2B valuation** tied to the $75M Series C.[1]  \n- Crunchbase News noted Calm was seeking to raise $150M at a **$2.2B valuation** in Oct 2020, though that appears to have been a reported negotiation rather than a completed round.[3]  \n- Sacra states: “Calm was last valued at **$2B** as of their 2020 Series C, which raised $75M” and describes the company as last valued at **$2B (2022)** with a total of $218M raised.[14]  \n- GetLatka lists a **$2B valuation**, noting Calm reached that valuation in **2025**, apparently referencing the same 2020 round and subsequent reporting, not a new priced round.[5]  \n\nBest supported answer: Calm’s **last disclosed primary-round valuation** is **$2.0B** (Series C, 2020). Later sources (Sacra, GetLatka) continue to use **$2B** as the reference valuation up through 2022–2025.[1][5][14]\n\n---\n\n## 2) Team size / headcount\n\nHeadcount numbers for Calm are based on third‑party data vendors scraping LinkedIn and other sources and should be treated as **estimates**:\n\n- Unify (an employee data/trends site) lists **426 total employees**, with a note indicating a **7.2% change from last year**.[10]  \n  URL: `https://www.unifygtm.com/insights-headcount/calm`\n- Highperformr.ai, another headcount aggregation site, lists **769 total employees** and then shows regional/department breakdowns summing to around **407 employees**, most in the US.[11]  \n  URL: `https://www.highperformr.ai/headcount/calm-com`\n\nThese numbers conflict; Unify shows ~400+ employees, Highperformr shows ~700+ headline with ~400 detailed. Both are **unofficial estimates** based on public profiles and not company‑reported HR data.[10][11]\n\nA reasonable takeaway is that Calm’s **current headcount is likely in the low‑hundreds (roughly 400–500 employees)**, but the exact number is uncertain and should be treated as an estimate drawn from these sources.[10][11]\n\n---\n\n## 3) Estimated downloads and revenue (Sensor Tower, Appfigures, data.ai – all estimates)\n\nThese analytics firms model usage based on store rankings/revenue and are explicitly **estimates**, not official Calm disclosures.\n\n**Appfigures (mental health vertical, April 2024)**\n\n- Appfigures reports that in **April 2024**, **Calm saw an estimated 508K downloads**.[7]  \n- The same report notes that Calm’s **revenue is up ~30% vs 2023**, though it also mentions demand for mental health apps is at an “all‑time low” and that peaks in revenue are less pronounced.[7]  \n- Appfigures explicitly states: “All figures included in this report are **estimated**… revenue is always net, meaning it’s the amount the developer earned after Apple and Google took their fee.”[7]\n\nURL: `https://appfigures.com/resources/insights/20240510?f=3`[7]\n\n**Sensor Tower (Q2 2024 performance analysis)**\n\nA Sensor Tower blog on top wellness apps in Q2 2024 describes Calm’s weekly metrics:\n\n- **Weekly revenue**: Calm started the quarter at about **$1M in weekly revenue** and ended around **$752K per week**.[15]  \n- **Downloads**: Weekly downloads declined from **~79K** at the start of the quarter to **~60K** towards the end.[15]  \n- **Weekly active users (WAU)**: Calm peaked at **1.5M weekly active users mid‑quarter** and then settled at about **1.3M WAU**.[15]  \n\nAll of those are explicitly performance analytics, and Sensor Tower’s numbers are understood to be **estimates** based on app store signals.[15]\n\nURL: `https://sensortower.com/blog/2024-q2-unified-top-5-wellness%20apps-revenue-us-63e274e1e1714cfff10eaa2f`[15]\n\n**data.ai**\n\nI did not surface a specific data.ai page in the prior search step; any data.ai numbers you use will need to be cross‑checked separately. For this answer, I’ll avoid fabricating data.ai numbers and instead rely on Appfigures and Sensor Tower above.\n\n---\n\n## 4) Business model and pricing tiers (Calm Premium, Calm Business, Calm Health)\n\nCalm’s **business model** is a mix of **direct‑to‑consumer subscription**, **enterprise/B2B**, and **healthcare‑oriented distribution**.\n\n**Consumer side – Calm Premium**\n\n- CarePaths’ 2026 Calm app review describes Calm as “the #1 app for sleep, meditation, and relaxation,” offering **guided meditations, Sleep Stories, breathing exercises, focus music, soundscapes, gentle movement, and Masterclasses**.[13]  \n- The review references **Calm Premium** as the main subscription unlocking full access to content, though it focuses less on the pricing details and more on the feature set.[13]\n\nURL: `https://carepaths.com/calm-app-review/`[13]\n\nStandard Calm Premium pricing in the stores (outside this response) is typically around an annual subscription; you should confirm exact prices directly in the App Store/Google Play because Calm frequently experiments with local pricing and promotions. In this answer I won’t quote store pricing to avoid using unsourced numbers.\n\n**Calm Business**\n\n- While not deeply detailed in the sources above, Calm Business is its **B2B offering** selling Calm seats to employers as an employee wellness benefit. You’ll typically see this marketed on Calm’s own website as a corporate program that gives workers access to Calm’s library plus engagement/analytics for HR teams. That’s based on common B2B wellness GTM patterns and Calm’s public positioning, but explicit details are not in the sources we surfaced. Any specific pricing tiers for Calm Business (per‑employee per‑month, minimum seat counts) should be verified directly on Calm’s enterprise page or via sales.[inference]\n\n**Calm Health**\n\n- PitchBook and Sacra note Calm as a “generating revenue” company with multiple later‑stage VC rounds and a grant in April 2022, which aligns with Calm’s push into **health‑adjacent offerings**.[8][14]  \n- Calm Health is generally described externally as a **healthcare‑integrated product** (e.g., offered via payers/providers) that bundles Calm’s content into clinical or health plan experiences. Specific pricing or reimbursement structures are not detailed in the sources surfaced and thus should be checked directly via Calm Health’s own materials or payer/provider documentation.[inference]\n\nBecause exact price points for Calm Premium, Calm Business, and Calm Health were not in the surfaced pages, it’s safer to treat pricing as **dynamic and location‑dependent**, and to rely on Calm’s own app store listings and sales materials for precise numbers.[inference]\n\n---\n\n## 5) 2024–2026 news: growth, layoffs, pivots, acquisitions, product launches\n\nFrom the sources surfaced:\n\n**Growth & revenue trajectory**\n\n- Sacra notes Calm had **2020 revenue of about $300M**, and ties that to the $2B valuation, implying a revenue multiple of around **6.67x**.[14]  \n  URL: `https://sacra.com/c/calm/`\n- GetLatka’s profile lists **estimated ARR (annual recurring revenue)** of **$596.4M in 2024** and continues to show a **$2B valuation**.[5] These numbers are model‑based and should be treated as **estimates**, not official guidance.\n  URL: `https://getlatka.com/companies/calm`\n- Appfigures notes Calm’s **net revenue up ~30% vs 2023** for mental health apps, although it also highlights that overall demand for mental health apps is at a low.[7]\n\nTaken together, these suggest **continued growth in subscription revenue** through 2024, but with **softening user acquisition and download volumes** consistent with broader mental‑health app sector trends.[5][7][14][15]\n\n**Layoffs / pivots / acquisitions**\n\nNone of the surfaced sources explicitly documented **2024–2026 layoffs or major pivots/acquisitions**. PitchBook does list secondary transactions, grants, and later‑stage VC rounds, but not M&A activity.[8]  \nURL: `https://pitchbook.com/profiles/company/56204-65`\n\nGiven that I can’t fetch additional news in this turn, I can’t reliably confirm or deny specific layoff events or acquisitions. You should check recent business press (e.g., TechCrunch, WSJ) and Calm’s own press page for 2024–2026 HR or M&A news.\n\n**Product launches**\n\n- CarePaths’ 2026 review emphasizes Calm’s expanded content set: **Masterclasses**, **movement sessions**, and a **broader music/soundscapes library**, framing this as Calm’s current, evolved product mix in 2026.[13]  \n- The Calm Music help article (updated 2025) indicates Calm has formalized a structured **Music** section with multiple sub‑categories (see next section), which reflects a product emphasis on **focus and soundscape‑based listening** beyond just guided meditation.[12]\n\nURLs:  \n- `https://carepaths.com/calm-app-review/`[13]  \n- `https://support.calm.com/hc/en-us/articles/115005144193-Calm-Music`[12]\n\n---\n\n## 6) Focus/work/study music and soundscape audio features: what they are and how prominent\n\nCalm’s **Music** and **soundscape** features are now a significant component of the app, positioned alongside meditation and Sleep Stories.\n\n**Official Calm Music help documentation**\n\nThe English Calm Music help center article (updated 2025) describes:\n\n- Calm Music contains **music tracks of various lengths**, “specially curated for Calm.”[12]  \n- Tracks are organized into categories including:\n  - **“Focus and Flow”**\n  - **“Uplift”**\n  - **“Piano”**\n  - **“Ambient and Atmospheric”**\n  - **“Electronic”**\n  - **“Classical and Strings”**\n  - **“Soundscapes”**[12]  \n- Users access Calm Music via the **“Music” button under the ‘Discover’ menu** in the app.[12]\n\nURL: `https://support.calm.com/hc/en-us/articles/115005144193-Calm-Music`[12]\n\nThe Korean‑language version of the Calm Music article similarly lists categories like **Focus**, **Lullaby**, **Relax**, **Sleep**, **Soundscape**, **Nature’s Melody**, confirming that **soundscapes and focus tracks are core named categories**, not minor add‑ons.[9]  \nURL: `https://support.calm.com/hc/ko-kr/articles/115005144193-Calm-%EC%9D%8C%EC%95%85`[9]\n\n**External review detail on music/soundscapes**\n\nCarePaths’ 2026 Calm app review provides more qualitative detail:\n\n- The review notes Calm offers **“focus music, soundscapes… curated audio designed for focus, relaxation, and sleep”**.[13]  \n- It explains that Calm’s music library is **“categorized by purpose (Focus and Flow, Uplift) and by genre (Piano, Ambient and Atmospheric, Electronic, Classical and Strings)”**, matching the Calm Music help center categories.[13][12]  \n- Calm includes **original compositions, exclusive tracks, and collaborations** with artists like **Moby** (exclusive ambient album) and **Alanis Morissette** (meditation album), making music a branded content differentiator rather than generic playlists.[13]  \n- The review describes **focus music** as having **steady rhythms** that support concentration without drawing attention away from work, and **relaxation music** as slower (around **60 BPM**) to help slow heart rate and breathing.[13]  \n- The **soundscapes** section is said to provide **high‑quality recordings of natural environments** that can be played on a loop.[13]\n\nURL: `https://carepaths.com/calm-app-review/`[13]\n\n**Prominence in the app**\n\nGiven:\n\n- There is a dedicated **Music button** in the **Discover** menu.[12]  \n- Multiple categories specifically target **focus/work/study use‑cases** (“Focus and Flow”, “Uplift”).[12][13]  \n- Calm positions its offering as including **focus music and soundscapes** in core product descriptions.[13]\n\nIt is reasonable to say Calm’s **focus/work/study audio and soundscapes are a prominent, first‑class feature set**, not a niche or hidden add‑on. They are one of the three main content pillars: **meditations**, **Sleep Stories**, and **music/soundscapes**.[12][13]\n\n---\n\n## 7) Reddit and forums: complaints about v7 app update and soundscapes/music features\n\nIn the prior search step, I attempted to surface Reddit/forum threads about **Calm’s v7 app update** and complaints about soundscapes/music, but I did not get specific Reddit URLs in the results list. Since I can’t perform new searches in this turn, I must be careful not to invent discussion threads.\n\nWhat I can say reliably:\n\n- It is common for major app redesigns (e.g., a jump to **version 7**) to provoke user complaints on Reddit and app‑store reviews around:\n  - Changes to navigation (e.g., difficulty finding the **Music** or **Soundscapes** sections after a redesign).[inference]\n  - Perceived removal or re‑labeling of favorite tracks or playlists.[inference]\n  - Pushes toward more aggressive upsells or paywalls (e.g., previously free soundscapes becoming Premium‑only).[inference]\n  - Performance issues or bugs (e.g., looping soundscapes stopping, cross‑fade issues).[inference]\n\nHowever, without direct Reddit URLs or captured content from the earlier tool call, I cannot attribute specific complaints or quotes. To get the **granular sentiment** you’re looking for (e.g., “v7 made the app unusable for soundscapes”, “music section buried under Discover”), you will need to:\n\n- Search **Reddit** for queries like `Calm app update v7`, `Calm soundscapes removed`, `Calm music section`, `Calm app update 2024` and read threads in r/Meditation, r/Calm, r/selfimprovement, r/mentalhealth.[guidance]\n- Cross‑check **App Store / Google Play reviews**, filtering by most recent and by version, to see common complaints about UI changes, content access, and audio behavior.[guidance]\n\nBecause I lack those specific threads in this turn, I cannot provide Reddit URLs or quote posts directly without risking fabrication, and I want to avoid that.\n\n---\n\n## Where to go next / what to verify\n\nFor an investor‑grade or product‑strategy analysis, I’d recommend:\n\n- **Funding/valuation:** Use Crunchbase, PitchBook and Sacra concurrently; treat **$217–218M raised** and **$2B valuation (2020 Series C)** as the best anchored numbers.[1][5][8][14]\n- **Headcount:** Use Unify and Highperformr as **ballpark indicators** (~400–500 employees), but confirm via LinkedIn or direct company statements.[10][11]\n- **Usage & revenue:** Use **Appfigures** for monthly downloads/revenue trends and **Sensor Tower** for weekly revenue/WAU/downloads; treat all numbers as **estimates**.[7][15]\n- **Product features:** Rely on Calm’s own **support articles** (Calm Music) plus independent reviews like CarePaths to understand the role of **focus music and soundscapes**.[9][12][13]\n- **User sentiment (v7, soundscapes/music):** Read **Reddit threads and app‑store reviews** directly; I can help synthesize them if you can supply links or content snippets in a future turn.\n\nIf you’d like, you can paste a few recent Reddit threads or App Store reviews about the v7 update and soundscapes, and I can systematically code complaints (e.g., navigation, content availability, audio quality) and map them to Calm’s product decisions.","refusal":null,"reasoning":null,"annotations":[{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/briefing-12-8-20/","title":"The Briefing: Calm Hits $2B Valuation With New Round, ...","start_index":0,"end_index":0}},{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/calm-raises-88m-series-b-to-become-mental-healths-first-unicorn/","title":"Calm Raises $88M Series B To Become Mental Health's ...","start_index":0,"end_index":0}},{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://news.crunchbase.com/venture/briefing-10-14-20/","title":"The Crunchbase News Briefing: Wed., Oct. 14","start_index":0,"end_index":0}},{"type":"url_cita